# The Bootstrap Graph Primer [SPEC:CXC-GRAPH-PRIMER]

> Every Intelligence spawned by `intelligence/launcher/monitor.py` arrived without the platform's
> capability knowledge, and behaved as you would expect: it forgot it could drive its own harness,
> forgot lts-engine was there to be queried, forgot the agent roster existed. It was not careless. It
> was never told. That knowledge lived in `bootstrap/toolbox.md` — *"The Architect's Resource
> Catalog"* — wired into two architect meta-objects and referenced by ZERO agent definitions anywhere.
> The framing was one role's; the content was everyone's.
>
> The fix is not to copy that file into more places, because a copy is what failed. So the primer is a
> set of graph node FAMILIES, fetched BY QUERY at every bootstrap — carrying ROUTING and never
> capability snapshots, and SCOPED so that no instance is handed a capability its host cannot serve.

- what-this-replaces: `bootstrap/toolbox.md`, retired into the primer per [CXC-PRIMER-TOOLBOX-DISPOSITION]. Its content was platform-generic; only its title and its wiring were the architect's.
- the-hinge: FETCH BY QUERY, never by id. A new capability becomes a new NODE, and no agent definition, meta-object or prompt line is edited to pick it up. Fetch-by-id would put an edit on every bootstrap for every addition — the maintenance burden that let the markdown catalog rot in the first place.
- the-shape: ONE core family every instance fetches, plus two orthogonal axes it selects on — HOST (where it runs) and DOMAIN (what it works on). Two or three cheap queries, names DERIVED from the instance's own identity so the hinge survives the extra families ([CXC-PRIMER-FAMILY-AXES]).
- invariant-one: ⛔ ROUTING, never per-agent capability lists. Anything with a live authoritative surface gets a POINTER, not a copy — [CXC-PRIMER-ROUTING-ONLY], earned by [CXC-PRIMER-STALE-COPY-INCIDENT].
- invariant-two: ⛔ CAPABILITIES ARE SCOPED, DISCIPLINE IS UNIVERSAL. Core carries how to think, how to verify and how to work with the human; a tool, service, namespace, path, version or machine is never core — [CXC-PRIMER-CORE-TEST], earned by [CXC-PRIMER-UNSCOPED-COPY-INCIDENT].
- the-two-halves-of-one-lesson: A STALE copy tells you something FALSE. An UNSCOPED copy tells you something TRUE THAT YOU CANNOT USE. Both are cost, and the second is harder to notice because it looks exactly like knowledge.
- ratified-by: David, 2026-08-13, on [DEC-GRAPH-PRIMER-FOR-ALL-BOOTSTRAPS] — families rather than one node, fetch-by-query rather than by id, `bootstrap/toolbox.md` retired; then the core-plus-two-axes family structure and the capabilities-are-scoped rule, ratified the same day.
- follow-on-deliverable: each family's own document, named but deliberately not authored here ([CXC-PRIMER-NOT-AUTHORED-HERE]).
- home-corpus: RATIFIED — ONE home for every family, in the versioned composeio repo ([DD-PRIMER-HOME-CORPUS]).
- governance-line: Routing is VERSIONED, data is NOT. Deployment corpora beyond composeio and factlattice must not be checked into github, so a family cannot live in one — and need not, because a member POINTS at a deployment's corpora and never contains them ([CXC-PRIMER-DEPLOYMENT-CORPORA]).

---

## 1. What actually failed

### It Was A DISTRIBUTION Failure, Not A Memory One [CXC-PRIMER-DISTRIBUTION-FAILURE]

- claim: The spawned Intelligences did not forget the harness surface, lts-engine or the agent roster. They were never given them. Diagnosed as an attention problem this produces reminders; diagnosed as a distribution problem it produces a channel, and only the second holds.
- evidence-the-content-was-generic: Every section of `bootstrap/toolbox.md` is platform knowledge, not role knowledge — the halt-vs-degrade-vs-stale classification, the MCP service table, the harness `{"cmd":...}` surface, the skills table, the agent roster. Nothing in it is about being an architect except its title.
- evidence-the-reach-was-not: `grep -rn toolbox .claude/agents/ ~/factlattice-deployment/agents/` returns 0 on 2026-08-13. The only consumers are `intelligence/meta/architect.meta.json`, `intelligence/launcher/architect.meta.sample.json` and the prompt/sensor code that reads the manifest key. Two meta-objects, both the architect's.
- why-the-shape-matters: A reminder scales with the instance count and decays with every one. A fetched node scales with nothing and decays never: there is one of it, and it is read rather than remembered.
- forcing-statement: David, 2026-08-13 — "intelligences spawned in intelligence/launcher/monitor.py were FORGETTING that they could call {\"cmd\":...} as well as many other capabilities, including lts-engine and the agent roster. Seems like part of all agentic bootstrapping should be to fetch a GRAPH PRIMER from lts-engine that includes whatever seems actually useful."
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-GRAPH-PRIMER]

### Carry-Depth-By-Reference Is MEASURED On A Cold Spawn, Not Assumed [CXC-PRIMER-FETCH-NEVER-COPY]

- claim: A cold child reliably performs a step that exists only in a spec its prompt CITES, so fetching works on exactly the population this primer targets — the instance holding nothing yet.
- measurement: [DEC-CITED-RITUAL-BOOTSTRAP-VERIFIED], 2026-08-13. A cold hosted child ran `lts_selfcheck` — a step present ONLY in the cited spec [SBR-PROCESS-FLOW] and nowhere in its own prompt. Two children, two reproductions.
- the-standing-rule-this-instantiates: [CXC-VISION-RECOVERY] — "carry the QUERY, never the snapshot", and [SPEC:CXC-VISION]'s own `how-to-use-this`: "FETCH it, never copy it. A static copy in an agent definition is the failure mode that node exists to prevent."
- what-the-prompt-may-carry: A pointer — one line naming the query. Never the content, never a summary, never a "for convenience" excerpt: an excerpt is a copy with a shorter half-life.
- the-bootstrap-paradox-and-its-resolution: The primer is what tells an instance lts-engine exists, so it cannot itself be fetched by an instance that learned about lts-engine from the primer. The prompt therefore carries the LITERAL query string, self-contained — the ONLY copy of anything primer-related outside the graph. One line, one file, and [CXC-PRIMER-GROWTH-TEST] proves it never changes as the primer grows.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-GRAPH-PRIMER]

---

## 2. The membership contract — seven families, fetched by QUERY

### The Query, Exactly [CXC-PRIMER-QUERY-SHAPE]

- query-core: `lts_find(corpus=<home>, function="bootstrap-core")` — issued by EVERY instance, always, unconditionally.
- query-host: `lts_find(corpus=<home>, function="bootstrap-" + <host>)` — issued when the instance has a host family, `<host>` taken from its own identity.
- query-domain: `lts_find(corpus=<home>, function="bootstrap-" + <domain>)` — issued when the instance has a domain family, `<domain>` taken from its own identity.
- membership-predicate: A node is a member of family F IF AND ONLY IF it carries the property `function` with the exact value F. Nothing else confers membership: not its id, not its file, not its section, not its document tag.
- single-membership-is-deliberate: `function` is exact string equality, so a node carries exactly ONE value and cannot be in two families. That is the point: a node wanting two homes has not been split along the core/scoped line yet ([CXC-PRIMER-CORE-TEST]), and genuine overlap gets a `see-also`, never a second membership ([CXC-PRIMER-CROSS-FAMILY-SHARING]).
- why-this-is-the-hinge: Fetch-by-id would require editing every bootstrap whenever a capability is added — precisely the maintenance burden that let `bootstrap/toolbox.md` rot. A query lets the primer grow without touching a single agent definition, meta-object or prompt line.
- engine-support-today: `factlattice_engine/tools.py:344` — `if function and str(props.get("function")) != str(function): return None`. Exact string equality against the node property. The MCP `lts_find` already exposes `function` (`factlattice_engine/server.py:153,186,296`). ZERO engine change; the facet has been callable all along.
- facet-is-unclaimed: `grep -rn "^- function:" corpora/` returns 0 across the whole ComposeIO corpus set on 2026-08-13, as does `^- service:`. No existing node can join by accident.
- a-zero-is-legible: `lts_find` returns `extent.keys_consulted`, which for these calls is `["function"]`. So `total_matched: 0` distinguishes an empty family from a mistyped facet — [CXC-TIER-TRUNCATION-IS-A-LIE]'s ambiguity, applied to recall. What a zero MEANS differs by family: see [CXC-PRIMER-ZERO-RESULT].
- verify-machine: the core query returns `total_matched >= 1` and `extent.keys_consulted == ["function"]`.
- verify-machine: every node in every result carries its family's `function` value as a PARSED property (read it back with `lts_node`, do not trust the source text).
- verify-machine: the union of all seven family queries equals the set of nodes carrying any `bootstrap-*` `function` value — no member is stranded in a family nothing queries.
- verify-human: David reads each returned set and confirms every member is something THAT instance needs at wake, and nothing else got in.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-GRAPH-PRIMER]

### One Core Plus Two Orthogonal Axes [CXC-PRIMER-FAMILY-AXES]

- ratified-shape: Seven families in one `function` vocabulary — a core, a HOST axis and a DOMAIN axis. Ratified by David 2026-08-13 in place of the single-value family this spec's first draft proposed.
- family-core: `bootstrap-core` — fetched by EVERY instance, always. How to think, how to verify, how to work with the human.
- axis-host: `bootstrap-ic`, `bootstrap-terminal`, `bootstrap-headless` — WHERE the instance runs, and therefore what machinery is actually listening for it.
- axis-domain: `bootstrap-cp`, `bootstrap-zb`, `bootstrap-haven` — WHAT the instance is working on, and therefore which corpora, services and conventions apply.
- axes-are-orthogonal: A `terminal` host working `cp`, and an `ic` host working `zb`, are both ordinary. Host does not imply domain and domain does not imply host — which is why they are two axes rather than one flattened list of situations.
- an-instance-fetches: core, plus its host family if it has one, plus its domain family if it has one — typically two or three queries, each cheap and each independently legible.
- the-vocabulary-is-open: The seven values are the ratified STARTING set, not a closed enum. A new host or domain is a new value, and [CXC-PRIMER-GROWTH-TEST] states the condition under which adding one still costs no code edit.
- naming-rule: `bootstrap-` + the axis token, lowercase, hyphenated. The prefix is what makes [CXC-PRIMER-QUERY-SHAPE]'s union check possible and stops the `function` key being colonised for unrelated purposes.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-GRAPH-PRIMER]

### Why `function=`, And Why Not `prefix=`, `text=` Or `area=` [CXC-PRIMER-FACET-CHOICE]

- decision: Select on the `function` property; reject the alternatives below, each for a measured reason.
- rejected-prefix: `prefix=` matches the node id's leading text, so membership would be welded to the id namespace. A primer node could then not ALSO be a spec node, a rename would silently drop a member, and a new top-level prefix needs a profile registration — David's ratify per [GUIDANCE-PREFIX-FIRST-SEGMENT]. With `function` a node keeps its registered `CXC` prefix and joins by property.
- rejected-text: `text=` is a SUBSTRING match over id, title and every property value (`_text_hit`), so it is over-inclusive (every node MENTIONING the primer matches) and under-inclusive (`find` never reads body prose — `extent.body_scanned: false`). A substring is not a membership predicate.
- rejected-area: `area` is a per-ROOT label assigned in `corpus-config.json` (`specs` / `decisions` / `artifacts`). Directory-scoped: it selects `corpora/platform-specs` whole and cannot select a subset. Too coarse.
- rejected-new-facet: Adding a `kind=` parameter to `lts_find` was considered and rejected: an engine change, a schema change and a deployment, for a filter that already exists under another name and is measured unused. `function` IS the `kind=` facet.
- the-property-name-is-not-descriptive-and-that-is-accepted: `function` reads oddly for "which family is this in". It is the exact-equality property facet the engine already ships; renaming it is a breaking change for cosmetic gain. Recorded so the next reader does not re-litigate it.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-GRAPH-PRIMER]

### Scoping The Query Is MANDATORY — An Unscoped Fetch Returns Every Member Three Times [CXC-PRIMER-SCOPE-MANDATORY]

- rule: The bootstrap query MUST pass `corpus=`. An unscoped `lts_find` iterates every loaded corpus, and `corpora/platform-specs` is a root of THREE, so every member returns once per corpus.
- measurement: `lts_find(prefix="SBR-TRUST")` with no `corpus` argument, run against the live engine 2026-08-13, returned `total_matched: 3` for ONE node — identical `content_hash`, identical `line_range`, differing only in the `corpus` field: `headless-monitor`, `intelligence-console`, `visual-composer`. Scoped to `corpus="intelligence-console"` it returns 1.
- consequence-if-ignored: A cold instance reads the whole primer three times and cannot tell: the duplicates are byte-identical and carry no flag. A silent 3x context tax on the one read every instance performs.
- root-cause: `corpus-config.json` registers `corpora/platform-specs` under `intelligence-console`, `visual-composer` AND `headless-monitor`. Deliberate sharing, not a defect; the query must account for it.
- secondary-defence: Dedupe on `id` regardless — a fourth corpus registering the same root would otherwise silently re-introduce the tax.
- corpus-id-is-not-the-directory-name: The corpus id is `intelligence-console`. There is no `platform-specs` corpus — a query naming it resolves to nothing and looks exactly like an empty family ([CXC-VISION-RECOVERY] records the same trap).
- verify-machine: the scoped query's `extent.corpora_consulted == ["intelligence-console"]` and no `id` appears twice in the result.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-GRAPH-PRIMER]

### Results Come Back ID-ALPHABETICAL — Reading Order Must Be Encoded, Not Assumed [CXC-PRIMER-ORDER]

- constraint: `lts_find` sorts its matches by `(corpus_id, node_id)` — `matches.sort(key=lambda cm: (cm[0], cm[1]["id"]))` in `factlattice_engine/tools.py`. Authored, file and section order are all discarded.
- consequence: A primer whose members must be read in a particular order cannot get that order from the query. If the halt-vs-degrade rule must land before the service routing, that is a fact about a property — never about the file.
- rule: Every member MUST carry `primer-order` with a zero-padded two-digit string (`"10"`, `"20"`, `"30"`, gaps of ten so a member can be inserted without renumbering). The consumer sorts on it. Ids SHOULD additionally be chosen so that alphabetical order and `primer-order` agree, so that a consumer which forgets to sort still gets a sane read.
- why-a-property-and-not-just-ids: Ids name what a node IS; renaming one to fix a sort position is how ids stop naming their subject. Order is a separate fact and gets a separate field.
- verify-machine: every member carries `primer-order`; the values are unique across the family; sorting the family by `primer-order` and by `id` yields the same sequence.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-GRAPH-PRIMER]

### The Growth Test — Add A Node, Edit Nothing Else [CXC-PRIMER-GROWTH-TEST]

- requirement-new-member: Adding a capability to an EXISTING family MUST require exactly one change: authoring a node carrying that family's `function` value into a document under a registered root. No agent definition, no meta-object, no prompt string, no code.
- requirement-new-family: Adding a whole new HOST or DOMAIN family MUST also require no code edit. This holds only if the family name is DERIVED — `"bootstrap-" + <host>` and `"bootstrap-" + <domain>`, read from the instance's own identity — and it FAILS the moment anyone writes a literal list of the seven values into the prompt builder.
- the-erosion-to-refuse: A hardcoded family list is the seductive version, because it looks like validation. It re-creates exactly the coupling fetch-by-query removes — a new deployment would need a prompt-builder edit before its instances could be primed. Names must be derived, and an unknown family must produce a legible zero, never a rejected query.
- why-this-is-the-acceptance-criterion-that-matters: Everything else here is machinery; this is what the machinery exists to produce. Its absence killed `bootstrap/toolbox.md` — that file was not wrong when written, it was expensive to keep right.
- verify-machine: record `total_matched` for a family query; author one new member; re-run the query STRING BYTE-FOR-BYTE unchanged; assert `total_matched` incremented by exactly 1 and the new id is present.
- verify-machine: author a member in a family name NO code has ever seen; assert an instance declaring that host or domain fetches it, with zero files edited outside `corpora/`.
- verify-machine: `git diff --name-only` for either change touches only files under `corpora/`; a hit outside `corpora/` fails the test.
- verify-machine: `grep -rn 'bootstrap-ic\|bootstrap-terminal\|bootstrap-headless\|bootstrap-cp\|bootstrap-zb\|bootstrap-haven' --include='*.py' .` returns ZERO — no family name but `bootstrap-core` may appear as a literal in code.
- verify-human: David adds a capability he thinks of himself, without consulting this spec, and confirms nothing else needed editing.
- anti-regression: These tests SHOULD live in the repo, not only here: the property is invisible until someone "just adds a line to the prompt too" and re-creates the coupling.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-GRAPH-PRIMER]

### The Family Has A Size Ceiling, Because It Is Read By Every Instance At Wake [CXC-PRIMER-BUDGET]

- rule-member: Each member MUST be at or under 800 tokens.
- rule-core-family: `bootstrap-core` MUST be at or under 4,000 tokens. It is the one family EVERY instance pays for, so it is held to the tightest ceiling.
- rule-scoped-family: Each host and each domain family MUST be at or under 3,000 tokens.
- rule-per-instance: The ceiling that actually matters is what ONE instance fetches — core plus one host plus one domain — which MUST be at or under 8,000 tokens. The per-family ceilings are set so that this holds by construction rather than by checking after the fact.
- why-the-instance-total-is-the-real-ceiling: The scoping ratify makes the whole primer bigger while making each instance's share smaller, so a ceiling on the WHOLE primer would now measure the wrong thing — it would penalise adding a family that no single instance ever pays for.
- why-800: 800 is p90 of node size measured over the live `intelligence-console` corpus (1,525 nodes, 560,132 tokens; p50 246, p75 480, p90 800, p95 1,019, p99 1,679). A member is a routing card, not an essay: past p90 it is carrying content that belongs behind a pointer.
- why-8000: The primer is paid for at EVERY wake, before the instance has done anything. 8,000 is under a quarter of `lts_find`'s 32,768 default budget, so two or three calls arrive with headroom and no caller reasons about paging on the boot path.
- the-failure-a-ceiling-prevents: An unbounded primer grows monotonically and is never noticed by the person adding to it, who only ever adds one node. The ceiling makes growth a decision rather than a drift.
- what-to-do-at-the-ceiling: Split a member and point, or retire one whose live home now covers it. Do NOT raise the ceiling first: raising it is a ratify and should feel like one.
- verify-machine: each family query at `tier="digest"` returns `token_count` per member; assert `max(token_count) <= 800`, `sum <= 4000` for core and `sum <= 3000` for each scoped family.
- verify-machine: for every (host, domain) pair in use, core + host + domain sums to at most 8,000, and each fetch completes in ONE call with `truncated: false`.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-GRAPH-PRIMER]

### `service=` Is RESERVED For Sub-Family Selection, And Deliberately Unused Today [CXC-PRIMER-SERVICE-RESERVED]

- reservation: `lts_find`'s `service` parameter is a second exact-equality property facet, identical in mechanics to `function` (`str(props.get("service")) != str(service)`), measured unused — 0 occurrences of `^- service:`.
- why-the-axes-did-not-use-it: The obvious reading of "two axes" is two keys — `function` for host, `service` for domain — which would let one node be in both. It was NOT built that way. David's ratify put both axes in ONE `function` vocabulary precisely so a node has exactly one home, and a node that seems to want two is a node whose content has not been split yet. Recorded because the two-key design is what the next reader will propose.
- intended-future-use: A SLICE within a family — a lightweight instance taking only part of core. `lts_find(corpus=..., function="bootstrap-core", service="minimal")` composes at no cost.
- why-reserve-rather-than-build: Nobody has needed a partial family yet, and a slice vocabulary invented before a consumer exists will be wrong. Reserving costs nothing and stops the next author reaching for a new facet.
- constraint-if-adopted: Every member of that family must then carry `service` — an absent property fails the equality test, so a partial rollout silently excludes members. Adopt per family in one edit or not at all.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-GRAPH-PRIMER]

---

## 3. The two invariants

### CAPABILITIES ARE SCOPED, DISCIPLINE IS UNIVERSAL [CXC-PRIMER-CORE-TEST]

- invariant: ⛔ `bootstrap-core` carries how to THINK, how to VERIFY and how to work with the HUMAN. What you can DRIVE, and what you are working ON, are scoped to a host or a domain family.
- the-test: Does the candidate name a TOOL, SERVICE, NAMESPACE, PATH, VERSION or MACHINE? Then it is NOT core — it is host or domain, and it MUST carry an `authority:` pointer. Does it name a WAY OF REASONING or a WORKING AGREEMENT? Then it IS core — and it carries NO `authority:`, because there is no live surface that could contradict it.
- why-mechanical-and-not-judgement: It resolves by asking what KIND OF NOUN the candidate contains, which two authors answer the same way. "Is this generally useful?" is the question that fails — everything in the retired catalog was generally useful, and that is how it ended up everywhere.
- the-machine-consequence: Presence of `authority` IS the core/scoped discriminator, so the split is checkable rather than asserted — see [CXC-PRIMER-AUTHORITY-PROPERTY]. Core with an `authority` and scoped without one are both errors, and they are the SAME error seen from two sides.
- candidate-core-content: Never trust an unguarded zero — re-run the query in a shape known to return something before believing a nothing. Report deltas, never absolutes. A true observation is not authorization: surface rather than override, and when unattended decide AND record. Never trust a signal produced inside a worktree about that worktree. Reading restores about 10% while reasoning restores about 40%.
- why-those-qualify: None names a tool, and each is true for an instance in ZB, in Haven, or in a bare terminal. The test passing, shown rather than argued.
- this-list-shapes-rather-than-enumerates: Illustrative of the SHAPE core takes, not the family's contents — those are authored elsewhere ([CXC-PRIMER-NOT-AUTHORED-HERE]) and governed by the test, not by this list.
- verify-machine: no member of `bootstrap-core` carries `authority`; every member of a host or domain family does.
- verify-human: David reads the core family and confirms every member would still be true for an instance on a machine that has never heard of ComposeIO.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-GRAPH-PRIMER]

### The Second Incident — A Capability Handed To An Instance That Could Not Use It [CXC-PRIMER-UNSCOPED-COPY-INCIDENT]

- incident: The harness `{"cmd":"context"|"compact"|"mcp"}` surface was argued into `bootstrap-core` on the reasoning that "every instance has a harness". That is FALSE. The mechanism requires the launcher's `HarnessMonitor` listening on the instance's out channel; a top-level terminal session has no monitor, so the capability does not apply to it AT ALL.
- david-2026-08-13: "it's only relevant if you're actually an agent instantiated in the console widget. You aren't right now, you don't need it. It's wasting your tokens."
- what-had-been-happening: An instance was carrying and REASONING ABOUT a capability it could not use, because the retired catalog handed it over at wake with nothing saying who it was for. Paid every wake, by every instance it does not apply to.
- why-it-is-harder-to-catch-than-a-stale-copy: A stale copy states something FALSE, so reality can eventually contradict it. An unscoped copy states something TRUE that this reader cannot act on — so it survives every accuracy check there is, because it is accurate. It looks exactly like knowledge.
- the-two-halves: [CXC-PRIMER-STALE-COPY-INCIDENT] is the FALSE half, and cost a working agent two capabilities. This is the TRUE-BUT-INAPPLICABLE half, and costs context at every wake forever. One disease — content separated from who it is for — answered structurally by families rather than by remembering to caveat.
- the-resulting-placement: `{"cmd":...}` belongs in `bootstrap-ic`, and in any other host family whose host runs a monitor, carrying its precondition explicitly rather than as an assumption.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-GRAPH-PRIMER]

### Shared Content Gets A Pointer Between Families, Never A Core Entry [CXC-PRIMER-CROSS-FAMILY-SHARING]

- rule: Where content is genuinely shared between two families — `ic` and `cp` will overlap, since the Intelligence Console is itself a ComposePlatform artifact — use a `see-also` pointer from one family's member to the other's. Never a copy, and never promotion into core.
- why-not-promote-to-core: Promotion is the tempting move and is [CXC-PRIMER-UNSCOPED-COPY-INCIDENT] exactly. Two families sharing something does not make it universal; it makes it shared by two. Promotion charges every instance to save a pointer for two.
- why-not-copy: A copy in two families is two things that can disagree, with nothing that notices — the same failure as the retired catalog, at smaller scale and with a longer fuse.
- which-family-owns-it: The one whose `authority` surface is the real one. `ic` owns a console mechanism even when `cp` cites it, because the console's code is what would change.
- same-rule-as-authority: `authority` points at the live surface rather than copying it; `see-also` says the same when that surface is another primer member. One discipline, two ranges.
- verify-machine: no two members of different families share a normalised body; a duplicate is a defect, not a convenience.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-GRAPH-PRIMER]

### ROUTING, NEVER PER-AGENT CAPABILITY LISTS [CXC-PRIMER-ROUTING-ONLY]

- invariant: ⛔ The primer carries ROUTING. It MUST NOT carry per-agent capability lists, tool inventories, or any claim about what a named agent, service or harness CAN and CANNOT do.
- the-durable-half: "factlattice-editor writes specs" is routing. True across roster changes, and what a lost instance actually needs.
- the-perishable-half: "factlattice-editor has no Bash" is a snapshot of a roster that changes, and it was FALSE within days of being written.
- the-generalisation: Anything with a live authoritative surface gets a POINTER, never a copy. For an agent that is its own `tools:` frontmatter; for the lts-engine tool set, `lts_capabilities`; for the harness commands, the HarnessMonitor in `intelligence/launcher/monitor.py`; for registered corpora, `lts_list_corpora`. The primer says WHERE TO LOOK and stops.
- the-test-a-candidate-line-must-pass: Could the sentence become false without anyone editing the primer? If yes it is a snapshot, and it becomes a pointer to whatever would have changed.
- verify-machine: no member's property values match the guard regex `(has|holds|lacks|cannot|does not have)\s+(no\s+)?(Bash|Write|Edit|Read|mcp__\\w+)` — a deliberately NARROW guard that catches the exact shape that already burned us and makes no claim to catch paraphrase.
- verify-human: David reads every member and asks of each line "could this be false tomorrow without anyone noticing?" — this is the real gate; the regex is a tripwire, not a proof.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-GRAPH-PRIMER]

### Every Member Names The Live Surface That Outranks It [CXC-PRIMER-AUTHORITY-PROPERTY]

- rule-scoped: Every member of a HOST or DOMAIN family MUST carry `authority` naming the live surface a reader consults when the member and reality disagree — a file path, a tool name, a service, or a node id.
- rule-core: Every member of `bootstrap-core` MUST NOT carry `authority`. There is no live surface that could contradict a way of reasoning, so an `authority` on a core member is the tell that the member is really a scoped one wearing the wrong family.
- the-discriminator: Presence of `authority` therefore IS the core/scoped boundary, made machine-readable. The [CXC-PRIMER-CORE-TEST] question — does this name a tool, service, namespace, path, version or machine — is answered by whether the author could name an authority at all.
- the-literal-self-is-retired: This spec's first draft permitted `authority: self` for a ratified rule with no other home. That is superseded: such a member is core, and core carries no `authority`. Recorded rather than silently dropped, because `self` was a real value in the draft the architect read.
- why-this-is-more-than-bookkeeping: It converts both invariants from instructions into a field. An author who cannot fill in `authority` has discovered the member is core; one who fills it in on a core member has discovered it is not. Either way the discovery lands while writing, not the morning someone believes it.
- it-also-tells-the-reader-what-to-do-when-it-is-wrong: A stale line with an `authority` is a two-second recovery; without one it is indistinguishable from a current one — [CXC-VISION-RECOVERY]'s named disease.
- verify-machine: 100% of host and domain members carry a non-empty `authority`; 0% of core members carry one. Both halves are asserted, because checking only the first would let core silently absorb scoped content.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-GRAPH-PRIMER]

### The Measured Cost Of One Stale Copy — It REMOVED Function From A Running Agent [CXC-PRIMER-STALE-COPY-INCIDENT]

- incident: `bootstrap/toolbox.md:177` asserted that `factlattice-editor` "has no Bash, so it cannot commit" and that its lint verdict was "a MANUAL rule application". Both were false: the agent's frontmatter grants Bash AND `mcp__lts-engine__lts_lint`.
- propagation: The architect read that line at wake, believed it, and wrote BOTH claims into a dispatch brief as explicit PROHIBITIONS — forbidding a running agent two capabilities it held. The brief that dispatched the authoring of this very document originally carried both.
- why-this-is-worse-than-a-missing-file: An absent catalog leaves an instance under-informed, and an under-informed instance asks. A stale one leaves it CONFIDENTLY WRONG, and a confident instance instructs. The copy did not fail to add function; it SUBTRACTED it.
- blast-radius-if-unfixed: Every instance bootstrapping from that file inherits the same prohibition, and none has a reason to check.
- the-rule-it-earned: [CXC-PRIMER-ROUTING-ONLY]. Recorded as an incident rather than summarised into the rule: a rule with its incident attached survives a rewrite that a bare rule does not.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-GRAPH-PRIMER]

---

## 4. What the primer carries

### The Charter — Named Here, Authored Elsewhere [CXC-PRIMER-CHARTER]

- thesis: The charter names the members each family starts with, so the split can be reviewed before any of it is written. It is split by family below because "what does core carry" and "what does `ic` carry" are separately-cited questions with separate authors.
- what-moved-and-why: `harness-surface` and `reconnect-limit` were core in this spec's first draft and are now `ic`, because both name a monitor that not every host runs. That correction is the whole of [CXC-PRIMER-UNSCOPED-COPY-INCIDENT].
- unpopulated-at-ratify: `bootstrap-terminal`, `bootstrap-headless`, `bootstrap-zb` and `bootstrap-haven` start empty. An empty family is legitimate and its query returns a legible zero rather than a fault ([CXC-PRIMER-ZERO-RESULT]).
- membership-is-not-frozen: This is the CHARTER; the DEFINITION is [CXC-PRIMER-QUERY-SHAPE] and the assignment rule is [CXC-PRIMER-CORE-TEST]. A member added later is a member — no charter node needs editing for any family to grow, and where charter and query disagree the query wins.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-GRAPH-PRIMER]

### Charter — `bootstrap-core` [CXC-PRIMER-CHARTER-CORE]

- halt-degrade-stale: INCORRECT (up, serving wrong) halts and is fixed; STALE (up, serving old) is fixed and NEVER routed around; UNAVAILABLE (genuinely absent) is the ONLY degrade, and degrades LOUDLY with the fallback named. A way of classifying a failure, naming no service.
- critical-absence-is-a-hard-stop: A MISSING critical resource is a hard stop, not a degrade — missing and broken are different failures. The RULE is core; WHICH resources are critical names services, and belongs to a host or domain family.
- lint-is-evidence-not-opinion: Run the checker yourself; an agent's "PASS" is a claim and a tool's output is evidence. It survives the roster correction that killed the table around it because it is about evidence discipline, not about who holds which tool.
- never-trust-an-unguarded-zero: Before believing a nothing, re-run the query in a shape known to return something. An unguarded zero is indistinguishable from a broken instrument.
- report-deltas-not-absolutes: An absolute invites the reader to assume a baseline; a delta states one.
- observation-is-not-authorization: Surface a changed fact pattern rather than overriding a ruling; unattended, decide AND record, then surface on return.
- worktree-signals-are-suspect: Never trust a signal produced inside a worktree about that worktree.
- read-versus-reason: Reading restores about 10%; reasoning restores about 40%. What that implies for a wake read is a reasoning move, not a tool.
- no-authority-on-any-of-these: None carries `authority`, and that is the test passing rather than an omission — not one of them names a tool, and every one holds for an instance on a machine that has never heard of ComposeIO.
- part-of: [CXC-PRIMER-CHARTER]

### Charter — the HOST axis [CXC-PRIMER-CHARTER-HOST]

- ic-harness-surface: The `{"cmd":"context"|"compact"|"mcp"|...}` directives an instance issues on its own `out` channel, including `{"cmd":"mcp","args":"reconnect lts-engine"}`. ⛔ PRECONDITION, stated on the member and never assumed: this requires the launcher's `HarnessMonitor` listening on the out channel. An instance without one cannot use any of it. `authority` = `intelligence/launcher/monitor.py`.
- ic-reconnect-limit: A reconnect re-attaches the CLIENT and does not restart a persistent daemon SERVER; a `corpus-config.json` change is served only after that daemon restarts. `authority` = `intelligence/launcher/monitor.py` plus the running daemon's own process table.
- terminal-and-headless: Empty at ratify. `bootstrap-terminal` is the family that would carry what a bare session CAN do, and its most valuable first member may be the statement that there is no monitor — the absence an instance currently has to infer.
- part-of: [CXC-PRIMER-CHARTER]

### Charter — the DOMAIN axis [CXC-PRIMER-CHARTER-DOMAIN]

- cp-lts-engine-reach: That lts-engine exists, that querying BEFORE re-deriving or re-asking is the reflex, and that its tool inventory is self-describing. `authority` = `lts_capabilities`; the primer names no individual tool.
- cp-agent-roster-routing: Which agent takes which kind of work, as ROUTING ONLY. `authority` = each agent's own `tools:` frontmatter. Under [CXC-PRIMER-ROUTING-ONLY] this member may not say what any of them CAN do.
- cp-build-pipeline: The station ORDER — narrative to spec-writer, lint as evidence, ratify, build, gap-analysis, adversarial QA, landing — durable even as station names change. `authority` = the pipeline's own spec.
- cp-worktree-write-lands-in-its-own-tree: A dispatched agent's `decision_ops` / `defect_ops` write lands in ITS OWN TREE — grep the live file after any agent reports a write. The general suspicion is core; THIS mechanism names two services, so it is domain. `authority` = [INSIGHT-WORKTREE-IS-NOT-THE-ENVIRONMENT].
- cp-skills-routing: The skills under `.claude/skills/` by name and by when-to-reach. `authority` = that directory.
- haven: Empty AND unreachable; the family name is reserved, not live. Both readings are dated measurements, held in [CXC-PRIMER-DEPLOYMENT-REACH] rather than restated here.
- the-family-lives-here-the-data-does-not: Both families live in the composeio repo with everything else ([DD-PRIMER-HOME-CORPUS]); their members POINT at deployment corpora deliberately kept out of github. Routing is versioned; data is not.
- the-ic-cp-overlap: The Intelligence Console is itself a ComposePlatform artifact, so `ic` and `cp` members will want to cite each other. That is a `see-also` between families and never a promotion into core ([CXC-PRIMER-CROSS-FAMILY-SHARING]).
- part-of: [CXC-PRIMER-CHARTER]

### The Family's Own Document Is Not Authored Here, And That Is Deliberate [CXC-PRIMER-NOT-AUTHORED-HERE]

- boundary: This document SPECIFIES the primer. It does not CONTAIN it. The charter members above are named and constrained; their text is a separate authoring deliverable.
- the-concrete-reason: Membership is by property, so any node here carrying `function: bootstrap-primer` would join the family. Co-locating governing document with governed content makes every illustrative example a live member and every editing slip a boot-path defect. This file names the facet dozens of times and MINTS it zero times — every occurrence is inside a property VALUE, never a key.
- where-it-goes: A dedicated document under `corpora/platform-specs/`, its numeric prefix allocated when written, not reserved here — that directory already carries TWO live numeric collisions (46 and 61), and a reservation would be the third.
- the-file-is-not-load-bearing: Nothing in the fetch contract names a file; the document need only sit under a root registered to the home corpus. Moving or renaming it changes nothing a bootstrap sees.
- verify-human: David accepts this split rather than expecting the primer text in this file.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-GRAPH-PRIMER]

---

## 5. Retiring `bootstrap/toolbox.md`

### Section-By-Section Disposition — Moves, Drops, Relocates [CXC-PRIMER-TOOLBOX-DISPOSITION]

- disposition-halt-degrade-stale: MOVES to the primer intact. Platform-generic, and a RULE rather than a snapshot. `authority` = [SBR-PROCESS-FLOW].
- disposition-availability-hard-stop: SPLITS. The classification RULE moves — missing-and-critical is a hard stop, missing-and-optional a note, present-but-broken routes to the halt/degrade rule. The CRITICAL / CRITICAL-DEGRADE / OPTIONAL service assignments RELOCATE to each role's definition: criticality is per-role, and `composeio-channel` is a hard stop for a bus-attached Intelligence and irrelevant to a worktree subagent.
- disposition-harness-commands: MOVES in substance, as the highest-value member. The `{"cmd":...}` shape and its three concrete forms are carried; `authority` becomes the HarnessMonitor source.
- disposition-slash-command-reference: DROPS. Four dense paragraphs pinned to "Claude Code 2.1.220" are a version-stamped copy of an external product's surface — copy-rot in its purest form, which the invariant forbids. What survives is the callable-by-you subset, already the harness member. The full table, if wanted, is a doc linking the vendor's current reference, not a boot-path read.
- disposition-mcp-service-table: SPLITS THREE WAYS. Which-service-for-what routing MOVES. The "if it's down" column COLLAPSES into the halt/degrade member where that rule lives. The per-service tool enumerations (`lts_find` / `lts_node` / `lts_traverse` / `decision_add` / ...) DROP in favour of a pointer to `lts_capabilities` and each server's own tool list — a live self-describing surface.
- disposition-worktree-warning: MOVES. Platform-generic, no other live home, and a failure mode with two recorded instances.
- disposition-skills-table: MOVES as routing (name plus when-to-reach), `authority` = `.claude/skills/`. No skill's behaviour is described.
- disposition-agent-tables: MOVES as ROUTING ONLY; every capability claim in both tables DROPS. This section produced [CXC-PRIMER-STALE-COPY-INCIDENT], and the CORRECTED entry drops with the wrong one — a correction is still a snapshot.
- disposition-lint-gate-callout: DROPS AS FALSE — the block asserting neither spec agent can execute a linter is measurably wrong for `factlattice-editor`. Its durable rule (run the tool yourself; an agent's PASS is an opinion) MOVES as `member-lint-is-evidence-not-opinion`, stripped of every claim about who holds what.
- disposition-spec-authoring-guidance: SPLITS. The PIPELINE ORDER moves (routing). "Your context is for reviewing a spec, not typing it" RELOCATES to the architect's role descriptor — one role's working discipline, not platform-generic, and already in that role's memory.
- disposition-maintenance-note: DROPS. A file's upkeep instruction is meaningless once the content is graph nodes; the primer's upkeep is [CXC-PRIMER-BUDGET] plus [CXC-PRIMER-AUTHORITY-PROPERTY].
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-GRAPH-PRIMER]

### Retirement Is Seven Code And Config References, Not A File Delete [CXC-PRIMER-TOOLBOX-RETIREMENT-ORDER]

- reference-inventory: `intelligence/meta/architect.meta.json` (the `toolbox` manifest key); `intelligence/launcher/architect.meta.sample.json` (same key); `python/composeio/ptycore.py` (the `_SECTIONS` entry and the consumption-contract comment); `python/composeio/self_sensor/sensor.py` (structural manifest-key detection); `python/tests/render/test_ptycore.py` (three assertions naming `toolbox`); `python/tests/render/test_registered_manifest_driven.py` (`_TOOLBOX = "bootstrap/toolbox.md"`); `python/composeio/render/widgets/intelligence_card.py` (a caption comment). Measured 2026-08-13.
- ordering: (1) author the primer family; (2) prove the query returns it; (3) add the unconditional prompt step [CXC-PRIMER-PROMPT-STEP]; (4) remove the `toolbox` entry from `_SECTIONS`; (5) remove the `toolbox` key from both meta-objects; (6) update the tests; (7) delete the file. Steps 1-3 are additive and reversible: nothing is removed until the replacement is proven live.
- the-existing-guard-that-makes-step-4-safe: `ptycore.unconsumed_manifest_keys()` reports any manifest key that is neither consumed by `_SECTIONS` nor listed in `DELIBERATELY_OMITTED`. Dropping `toolbox` from `_SECTIONS` at step 4 while it still sits in the manifests makes the prompt LOUDLY name it and fails a test. A half-finished retirement announces itself.
- do-not-move-the-file-into-the-role: The tempting shortcut — retitle `toolbox.md` as the architect's own document and leave it — reproduces the original defect with a smaller blast radius: generic content reachable by one role, and the architect still reading a copy.
- verify-machine: after step 7, `grep -rn "toolbox" --include='*.py' --include='*.json' --include='*.md' .` returns no hit resolving to `bootstrap/toolbox.md`; the full `python/tests/render` suite passes.
- verify-human: David confirms nothing he relied on in that file is unreachable — the disposition table is the checklist for that read.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-GRAPH-PRIMER]

