## 6. Wiring

### The Prompt Step Is UNCONDITIONAL — Never Manifest-Gated [CXC-PRIMER-PROMPT-STEP]

- requirement: `ptycore.build_bootstrap_prompt` MUST emit the CORE primer step for EVERY instance, unconditionally. It MUST NOT be driven by a manifest key.
- requirement-scoped-steps: The host and domain steps are emitted when the instance HAS a host or a domain, with the family name DERIVED from its identity ([CXC-PRIMER-GROWTH-TEST]). Absent axis, absent step — and that absence is a fact about the instance, not a manifest lookup that can silently miss.
- why-core-is-different-from-the-other-two: Core is unconditional because it is the guard ([CXC-PRIMER-ZERO-RESULT]) and because discipline is universal. The scoped steps are conditional because capabilities are not. That asymmetry is the ratified invariant expressed in the emission itself.
- why-this-is-the-load-bearing-half-of-the-wiring: Every `_SECTIONS` entry is looked up with `manifest.get(key)` and SKIPPED when absent. That skip is how `toolbox.md` reached only the architect — two manifests carried the key, no others did. A manifest-gated primer reproduces the original defect through the original mechanism.
- position: In the slot `toolbox` occupies today — after `method_flow`, BEFORE `bootstrap_read`. The reason is ratified in that table's own comment: the strategic read RUNS AGAINST these tools, so knowing what you can reach for precedes finding out what is true.
- emission-shape: A literal step naming the query, not a path. `_SECTIONS` maps to "READ <path>"; the primer maps to "RUN <query> and read what comes back". Write it as a different emission rather than bending it into the path-shaped table.
- what-the-line-may-contain: The query string, the instruction to read every returned node, and the zero-result rule. Nothing else — no summary, no "in particular note that...". Every extra word is a copy with a shorter half-life than the graph.
- verify-machine: `build_bootstrap_prompt({})` — an EMPTY manifest — contains the CORE primer step and its literal query string.
- verify-machine: `build_bootstrap_prompt` for every meta-object in `intelligence/meta/` emits the core step exactly once, positioned after METHOD FLOW and before THE STRATEGIC READ, with the scoped steps immediately after it and before the strategic read.
- verify-human: David reads a generated prompt end to end and confirms the steps are unmissable to a cold reader, and that a reader can tell which capabilities are conditional on its host.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-GRAPH-PRIMER]

### What An Empty Primer Result Means [CXC-PRIMER-ZERO-RESULT]

- claim: A zero on the CORE query is a hard stop. A zero on a host or domain query is legitimate, and must be announced rather than absorbed.
- rule-core: If `bootstrap-core` returns `total_matched: 0`, the instance MUST announce it LOUDLY and STOP, exactly as for any absent critical resource. It MUST NOT proceed un-primed and it MUST NOT fall back to reading a file.
- rule-scoped: If a host or domain query returns 0, the instance CONTINUES and SAYS SO — "no `bootstrap-zb` members" is a true and useful statement about an unpopulated family. Silence is the defect here, not the zero.
- the-core-query-is-the-guard: The primer applying its own core rule to itself. `never-trust-an-unguarded-zero` says re-run in a shape known to return something — and the core query IS that shape. A non-zero core proves in one call that the engine is reachable, the corpus loaded and the facet matching, so a later zero on a host family is a fact about the family, not the instrument. Core FIRST, always.
- why-not-a-file-fallback: There will be no file to fall back to — `bootstrap/toolbox.md` is retired. A fallback would re-create the second copy this design exists to eliminate, and a fallback silently taken is how an instance ends up believing a stale snapshot.
- why-zero-is-legible-at-all: `extent.keys_consulted` for these calls is `["function"]`, so a zero says the key was consulted and matched nothing. It cannot be confused with a key miss.
- distinguish-the-three-failures: Zero on CORE from a reachable engine is a corpus problem — missing or unregistered — and halts. Zero on a SCOPED family is an unpopulated family and is announced. An UNREACHABLE engine is the halt-vs-degrade case, also a hard stop. Three outcomes, three fixes; conflating any two was the original defect.
- verify-machine: an integration test points the CORE query at a corpus with no members and asserts the bootstrap path halts rather than continuing.
- verify-machine: a second test returns a non-empty core and an empty host family, and asserts the instance continues AND emits the unpopulated-family statement.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-GRAPH-PRIMER]

### The Home Corpus Is A Named Constant In ONE Place [CXC-PRIMER-HOME-CORPUS]

- rule: ONE `<home>`, in the versioned composeio repo, for EVERY family — core and scoped alike ([DD-PRIMER-HOME-CORPUS], ratified). It resolves in exactly one place in the codebase, the prompt-builder, and is never repeated in an agent definition, a meta-object or a role descriptor.
- why-a-constant-at-all: [CXC-PRIMER-SCOPE-MANDATORY] makes `corpus=` compulsory, so some corpus id must be named. Naming it once preserves the hinge: adding a MEMBER still needs no code edit; only moving the HOME would, and that is one line in one file.
- the-governance-reason-it-is-one: Primer families are bootstrap ROUTING, which is platform material and belongs in the versioned repo. A deployment's own data corpora must not be in github, so a family cannot live in one — see [DD-PRIMER-HOME-CORPUS]'s exclusion of option B, which is a constraint and not a preference.
- routing-lives-here-data-lives-there: The line that makes one home workable at all. A primer member NAMES a deployment's corpora; it never contains them. [CXC-PRIMER-DEPLOYMENT-CORPORA] is how it names them without reproducing them.
- what-does-not-change: The 3x-to-12x duplication argument in [CXC-PRIMER-SCOPE-MANDATORY]. Registering `corpora/platform-specs` as a root of every corpus stays ruled out, independently of this ratify.
- verify-machine: `grep -rn "bootstrap-core" --include='*.py' --include='*.json' --include='*.md' .` returns exactly one hit outside `corpora/`, and no scoped family name appears in code at all.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-GRAPH-PRIMER]

### How A Domain Family Names A Deployment's Corpora [CXC-PRIMER-DEPLOYMENT-CORPORA]

- rule: A domain family member MUST NOT reproduce a deployment's corpus inventory. It names the LIVE REGISTRY and a filter, so the set grows without editing the member or any consumer.
- forcing-fact: David, 2026-08-13 — "Note that ZB will have several corpora." A domain family therefore cannot assume one corpus per deployment, and the count is not stable.
- the-registry-is-queryable-so-point-at-it: `lts_list_corpora` is a live MCP tool returning, per corpus, `corpus_id`, `domain_suffix`, `root_paths`, node and edge counts and a `fresh` flag. So a `bootstrap-zb` member says "call `lts_list_corpora`, take every corpus whose `domain_suffix` begins `zb.`" — naming not one id.
- what-a-hardcoded-list-would-be: ⛔ A snapshot, rotting exactly as `bootstrap/toolbox.md` did and for the same reason — a hand-maintained inventory with nothing watching it. [CXC-PRIMER-ROUTING-ONLY] applied to corpora rather than agents; the same rule.
- authority: `lts_list_corpora`, and behind it the `FACTLATTICE_CORPUS_CONFIG` registry. That is the live surface, and a member that disagrees with it is wrong by construction.
- the-count-caveat-to-carry: `lts_list_corpora`'s counts are read off the last index build, so it reports `counts_agree` and a per-corpus `fresh` flag beside them. A member citing the registry cites the FLAG, never a bare count — the engine's own [FL-RECALL-INVISIBLE-PRECONDITIONS] lesson, which cost a 171-versus-184 discrepancy that travelled alone.
- verify-machine: no primer member's property values contain a corpus id other than the home; a grep for registered corpus ids across the primer families returns only `<home>`.
- verify-human: David confirms no primer member reproduces anything that `lts_list_corpora` already serves.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-GRAPH-PRIMER]

### Which Deployments Can Actually Be Reached Today [CXC-PRIMER-DEPLOYMENT-REACH]

- measured-on: 2026-08-13, against the live engine. Stated as a dated MEASUREMENT rather than a property of the design, because it is the half of [CXC-PRIMER-DEPLOYMENT-CORPORA] that goes stale.
- haven-is-not: There is NO Haven corpus in this engine. Twelve corpora are registered and none is Haven's, so Haven instances cannot bootstrap from this graph at all until one exists. `bootstrap-haven` is a name RESERVED for a deployment that is not yet present.
- why-that-distinction-is-worth-a-node: `zb` and `haven` look symmetric in the vocabulary and are not symmetric in fact. A `bootstrap-haven` family written today would hold members nothing can fetch — not empty but UNREACHABLE, a third state [CXC-PRIMER-ZERO-RESULT] cannot cover because it never arises from a query.
- invalidating_assumption: That Haven stays unregistered. The day a Haven corpus is registered, `bootstrap-haven` becomes fetchable and `haven-is-not` above is stale — re-measure with `lts_list_corpora` rather than trusting this line.
- how-to-re-measure: `lts_list_corpora` and read `corpora_available`. That is the live surface; this node is a reading of it, not a substitute for it.
- authority: `lts_list_corpora`.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-GRAPH-PRIMER]

---

## 7. Where the families live

### Where Each Family Lives [DD-PRIMER-HOME-CORPUS]

- decision: RATIFIED — option A. ONE home corpus for every family, in the versioned composeio repo. Every `bootstrap-*` family, core and scoped alike, lives in one corpus and is fetched with one `<home>`.
- status: ratified
- lifecycle-position: decided
- david-verbatim: "A — one home corpus for every family. The corpus data beyond composeIO and factlattice must not be checked into github. Note that ZB will have several corpora."
- dsd-source: docs/intelligence-console/DECISIONS-2026-08-13-crossing-primer-and-publication.md
- rationale: The governance constraint decides it. Primer families describe bootstrap ROUTING — platform material, belonging in the versioned, reviewable, lintable repo. A deployment's DATA corpora stay private and out of github, and nothing about the primer requires otherwise: a member POINTS AT a corpus, never contains one.
- alternative-b-is-excluded-not-outweighed: ⛔ B — each family in its own deployment's corpus — is IMPOSSIBLE under the constraint, not merely less preferred. ZB and Haven corpus data must not be in github, so a `bootstrap-zb` family living there would sit outside the versioned surface the primer's warrant rests on, and a family that cannot be linted in one pass cannot be warranted. Recorded as an EXCLUSION rather than a tradeoff, because the next reader will otherwise re-propose B on locality grounds and re-derive an argument already had.
- alternative-c-not-taken: A dedicated `primer` corpus. Not excluded by the constraint — it could live in composeio too — but it costs a registration plus a daemon restart per deployment and sites the primer where nobody browsing the specs will find it. A is cheaper for the same governance outcome.
- alternatives: A (ratified), B (excluded by governance), C (available, not taken).
- ruled-out-independently: Registering `corpora/platform-specs` as a root of every corpus. The measured 3x duplication becomes 12x on any unscoped query ([CXC-PRIMER-SCOPE-MANDATORY]), and that cost lands on every query in the system rather than on this one. Unchanged by the ratify and unaffected by it.
- invalidating_assumption: That routing and data stay separable — that no primer member ever needs to CONTAIN deployment content rather than point at it. [CXC-PRIMER-DEPLOYMENT-CORPORA] is what keeps that true, and if a member ever has to inline a private inventory, this ratify is back open.
- also-invalidated-by: A deployment whose instances cannot reach the composeio corpus at all. Haven is the live candidate — it has no corpus in this engine today, so the question is not yet askable of it.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-GRAPH-PRIMER]

---

## Relationships

### Semantic

